{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"1e0af9d96397dd2a","name":"card_event_swift_response","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/Homefront/events/card_event_swift_response","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SWIFT RESPONSE","text":"When a friendly unit is destroyed, your units get +1+1 and Shock.","flavorText":"On 23 December 1944, the skies over the Ardennes finally cleared after days of snow and fog. The German Ardennes Offensive, known as the Battle of the Bulge, had relied on poor weather to shield its forces from Allied aircraft. But with visibility restored, the US Eighth and Ninth Air Forces responded immediately. P-47 Thunderbolts and P-51 Mustangs scrambled from bases across France and Belgium, targeting German columns that had advanced deep into Allied lines. Many pilots were airborne within minutes of the order. Forward air controllers guided strikes with growing precision, directing aircraft to roads choked with vehicles. German tanks and trucks, often stuck in traffic or short on fuel, were ideal targets. By the end of the day, hundreds of sorties had hit supply lines, bridges, and convoys. The Luftwaffe, already depleted, was unable to contest the skies. The sudden and overwhelming air response crippled the momentum of the German offensive and the tide began to turn. This rapid arrival of air power not only halted further German advances but helped encircle key enemy formations. The 23 December counterstrikes marked a turning point in the battle, proving how quickly Allied air forces could exploit a change in weather to devastating effect.","faction":"USA","type":"gotcha","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_swift_response","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_swift_response_title","SourceString":"SWIFT RESPONSE","LocalizedString":"SWIFT RESPONSE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::gotcha","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":2,"Text":{"Names